<Project>

  <!--
    Where the TypeScript compiler comes from.

    The projects that build HTTPRoot scripts used to run a bare "tsc", which
    means the one installed globally on whichever machine is building. That is
    wrong twice over.

    It is not there. A fresh Debian with Node.js but no "npm install -g
    typescript" stops the build with

      error MSB3073: The command "tsc" exited with code 127

    which is the shell saying "no such command" dressed as an MSBuild error,
    arriving after a dozen projects have already reported success.

    And where it is there, it is whatever version that machine happens to
    have. The emitted .js files are checked in, so the version decides their
    bytes. Measured against date.format.js: 5.7.3, 5.8.3 and 5.9.3 reproduce
    what this repository had committed, while 6.0.3 prefixes "use strict" to
    it. WWCP_Core's committed output is the other way round - it came from a
    6.x compiler - so both repositories pin 6.0.3, and the one file that
    disagreed here was regenerated once and committed. It is a type
    declaration file whose entire emit is four comment lines, so the line it
    gained runs nothing.

    A build that changed nothing must leave the working tree clean, and since
    the build stamp that is doubly true: a tree git calls dirty is a build
    stamped "-dirty", and a marker nobody believes is worse than no marker.
    .gitattributes beside this file finishes that job - the compiler writes
    exactly the bytes that are committed, and core.autocrlf must not rewrite
    them on the way out.

    Only the projects that already ran tsc use this. Several others carry a
    tsconfig.json without ever having compiled it as part of a build, and
    turning that on now would rewrite files nobody asked about.
  -->

  <!--
    And the same for the stylesheets, which is the same fault twice.

    HTTPRoot/css is generated from the .scss files beside it, gitignored, and
    embedded as a resource. Six of these projects could therefore not be built
    at all on a machine where nobody had run compileSASS.sh:

      error CS1566: Fehler beim Lesen der Ressource
                    "...HTTPRoot.css.events.events.css"

    which names the missing file and not the missing step. A seventh -
    WWCP_OCPPv2.1_ChargingStation, the one this solution actually uses - built
    only because those files happened to be lying around from an earlier run.
    A fresh clone would not have had them, so it was one "git clean" away from
    the same failure.

    That script needed "sass" and "jq" installed globally, the same
    arrangement that made "tsc" fail, so it gets the same answer: sass pinned
    in package.json, the work in compileSASS.mjs at the repository root, and
    the build runs it. One copy rather than eleven beside the projects,
    because eleven copies of a thing this small are eleven chances to fix nine
    of them.

    NodeToolsRoot rather than TypeScriptRoot: it holds two tools now.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NodeToolsRoot>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)'))</NodeToolsRoot>
    <TypeScriptCompiler>$(NodeToolsRoot)node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc</TypeScriptCompiler>
    <StylesheetCompiler>$(NodeToolsRoot)compileSASS.mjs</StylesheetCompiler>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
